<spell-notebook "divider usage">
	<header level="1">
		Divider
		<header>A divider visually segments content into groups.</>
	</>
	<divider-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put `<ui-divider>` between two blocks of content. With no words it draws a rule; `direction="horizontal"` puts its text
			(and an `icon`) between two rules, `direction="vertical"` stands it upright between two columns.

			- `spacer` keeps the spacing without the line: Fomantic's `hidden` divider (its class word is still
			  `hidden`). It was renamed because `hidden` hides every element, as the platform's does.
			- `section` adds more space; `padding="none"` removes it; `clearing` clears floats above it.
			- `align="left|right"` on a horizontal divider drops the rule on that side.
			- A vertical divider centres itself in its nearest positioned owner: a `<ui-segment>`, a `<ui-grid>`. In a
			  `stackable` grid it turns horizontal when the grid stacks.
			- No events: a divider is static.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>A divider takes no focus and handles no keys.</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A divider is `role="separator"`, not `<hr>`, because a horizontal or vertical divider carries text an `<hr>`
			  can't hold.
			- A vertical divider adds `aria-orientation="vertical"`.
			- A `spacer` divider is `role="none"`: it is spacing only and is not announced.
			- The `icon` is decorative.
			- If the element fails to render, it shows its text through a bare `<slot>`: the text stays, the
			  divider line and the `icon` shorthand go.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
